<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-double-integrals-general-regions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Double Integrals Over General Regions and Area by Double Integration</title>

<p>
Here we will generalize our last session's discussion about double
integrals and how they can be used to evaluate volumes. Last session we
restricted ourselves to shapes with rectangular base. Today we will
look at more general shapes for the base.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-general-regions-case1">
<title>Case I: Regions of the Form <m>R = \{(x,y) : a \le x \le b,\ f_2(x) \le y \le f_1(x)\}</m></title>

<example xml:id="example-case1-vertical">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the following double integral,
</p>

<me>
\iint_R \left(2xy + 1\right)\,dx\,dy
</me>

<p>
where
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 1,\ (-2x^2+2x) \le y \le (x^3+1)\}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
The region <m>R</m> is shown in
<xref ref="fig-case1-region-prefigure"/>. Since for each fixed
<m>x</m> the variable <m>y</m> runs from the lower curve
<m>f_2(x) = -2x^2+2x</m> to the upper curve <m>f_1(x) = x^3+1</m>, we
integrate along the green vertical strips first. We can re-write the
integral as
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_0^1\!\!\int_{-2x^2+2x}^{x^3+1}
\left(2xy+1\right)\,dy\,dx
\amp= \int_0^1\left(\Big.\left(xy^2 + y\right)
\Big|_{-2x^2+2x}^{x^3+1}\right)dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^1\Big[\left(x(x^3+1)^2 + x^3 + 1\right)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\qquad - \left(x(-2x^2+2x)^2 - 2x^2 + 2x\right)\Big]\,dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^1\Big[\left(x^7 + 2x^4 + x + x^3 + 1\right)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\qquad - \left(4x^5 - 8x^4 + 4x^3 - 2x^2 + 2x\right)\Big]\,dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \left.\left(\frac{x^8}{8} + \frac{2}{5}x^5 + \frac{x^2}{2}
+ \frac{x^4}{4} + x\right.\right.
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\qquad \left.\left. - \frac{2}{3}x^6 + \frac{8}{5}x^5 - x^4
+ \frac{2}{3}x^3 - x^2\right)\right|_0^1
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \frac{15}{8}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-case1-region-prefigure">
<caption>
The region <m>R</m> of <xref ref="example-case1-vertical"/>, bounded
above by <m>f_1(x) = x^3 + 1</m> and below by
<m>f_2(x) = -2x^2 + 2x</m> for <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>. For each fixed
<m>x</m>, the inner integral runs along a vertical strip from the
lower curve to the upper curve.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-case1-region">
<diagram dimensions="(380,300)" margins="10">
<definition>f1(t) = (t, t^3 + 1)</definition>
<definition>f2(t) = (t, -2*t^2 + 2*t)</definition>
<definition>f1sc(t) = t^3 + 1</definition>
<definition>f2sc(t) = -2*t^2 + 2*t</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.4,1.45,2.45)">
<axes at="c1-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<area-between-curves at="c1-region" function1="f2sc" function2="f1sc"
    domain="(0,1)" fill="#d3e8d3" stroke="none"/>
<line at="c1-strip1" endpoints="((0.25,0.375),(0.25,1.0156))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c1-strip2" endpoints="((0.5,0.5),(0.5,1.125))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c1-strip3" endpoints="((0.75,0.375),(0.75,1.4219))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c1-left" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<line at="c1-right" endpoints="((1,0),(1,2))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="c1-f1" function="f1" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="c1-f2" function="f2" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="c1-f1-label" anchor="(0.42,1.35)" alignment="nw"
    color="#0072B2"><m>f_1(x) = x^3 + 1</m></label>
<label at="c1-f2-label" anchor="(0.52,0.28)" alignment="s"
    color="#D55E00"><m>f_2(x) = -2x^2 + 2x</m></label>
<point at="c1-x0" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</point>
<point at="c1-x1" p="(1,0)" fill="black" alignment="se">
<m>1</m>
</point>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The region between two curves above the interval from zero to one on the x axis. The upper boundary, drawn in blue, is f 1 of x equals x cubed plus one, rising from one to two. The lower boundary, drawn in vermillion, is f 2 of x equals negative two x squared plus two x, an arch from zero up to one half and back to zero. The region between them is shaded light green with three vertical green strips.">
  <annotation ref="c1-f1" text="The upper boundary f 1 of x equals x cubed plus 1, drawn in blue."/>
  <annotation ref="c1-f2" text="The lower boundary f 2 of x equals negative 2 x squared plus 2 x, drawn in vermillion."/>
  <annotation ref="c1-region" text="The region R, shaded in light green."/>
  <annotation ref="c1-strip2" text="A typical vertical strip at a fixed x. The inner integral in y runs along this strip from the lower curve to the upper curve."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-general-regions-case2">
<title>Case II: Regions of the Form <m>R = \{(x,y) : c \le y \le d,\ g_1(y) \le x \le g_2(y)\}</m></title>

<example xml:id="example-case2-horizontal">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the following double integral,
</p>

<me>
\iint_R x^2 y\,dx\,dy
</me>

<p>
where
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0.1 \le y \le 0.5,\ y^2 \le x \le y^{1/3}\}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
The region <m>R</m> is shown in
<xref ref="fig-case2-region-prefigure"/>. This time, for each fixed
<m>y</m>, the variable <m>x</m> runs along a horizontal strip from
<m>g_1(y) = y^2</m> to <m>g_2(y) = y^{1/3}</m>, so we integrate with
respect to <m>x</m> first:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_{0.1}^{0.5}\!\!\int_{y^2}^{y^{1/3}} x^2 y\,dx\,dy
\amp= \int_{0.1}^{0.5}
\left[\left.\left(\frac{1}{3}x^3 y\right)\right|_{y^2}^{y^{1/3}}
\right]dy
= \frac{1}{3}\int_{0.1}^{0.5}\left(y^2 - y^7\right)dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \frac{1}{3}\left.\left(\frac{y^3}{3}
- \frac{y^8}{8}\right)\right|_{0.1}^{0.5}
= 0.013615.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-case2-region-prefigure">
<caption>
The region <m>R</m> of <xref ref="example-case2-horizontal"/>, bounded
on the left by <m>g_1(y) = y^2</m> and on the right by
<m>g_2(y) = y^{1/3}</m> for <m>0.1 \le y \le 0.5</m>. For each fixed
<m>y</m>, the inner integral runs along a horizontal strip from the
left curve to the right curve.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-case2-region">
<diagram dimensions="(380,300)" margins="10">
<definition>g1(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<definition>g2(t) = (t^(1/3), t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.12,-0.09,1.22,0.68)">
<axes at="c2-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<polygon at="c2-region" fill="#d3e8d3" stroke="none" closed="yes"
    points="((0.01,0.1),(0.01361,0.1167),(0.01778,0.1333),(0.0225,0.15),(0.02778,0.1667),(0.03361,0.1833),(0.04,0.2),(0.04694,0.2167),(0.05444,0.2333),(0.0625,0.25),(0.07111,0.2667),(0.08028,0.2833),(0.09,0.3),(0.1003,0.3167),(0.1111,0.3333),(0.1225,0.35),(0.1344,0.3667),(0.1469,0.3833),(0.16,0.4),(0.1736,0.4167),(0.1878,0.4333),(0.2025,0.45),(0.2178,0.4667),(0.2336,0.4833),(0.25,0.5),(0.7937,0.5),(0.7848,0.4833),(0.7757,0.4667),(0.7663,0.45),(0.7567,0.4333),(0.7469,0.4167),(0.7368,0.4),(0.7264,0.3833),(0.7157,0.3667),(0.7047,0.35),(0.6934,0.3333),(0.6816,0.3167),(0.6694,0.3),(0.6568,0.2833),(0.6437,0.2667),(0.63,0.25),(0.6156,0.2333),(0.6006,0.2167),(0.5848,0.2),(0.5681,0.1833),(0.5503,0.1667),(0.5313,0.15),(0.5109,0.1333),(0.4886,0.1167),(0.4642,0.1))"/>
<line at="c2-strip1" endpoints="((0.04,0.2),(0.585,0.2))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c2-strip2" endpoints="((0.09,0.3),(0.669,0.3))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c2-strip3" endpoints="((0.16,0.4),(0.737,0.4))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c2-bottom" endpoints="((0.01,0.1),(0.464,0.1))"
    stroke="black" thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<line at="c2-top" endpoints="((0.25,0.5),(0.794,0.5))"
    stroke="black" thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="c2-g1" function="g1" domain="(0.1,0.5)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="c2-g2" function="g2" domain="(0.1,0.5)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<label at="c2-g1-label" anchor="(0.02,0.56)" alignment="ne"
    color="#D55E00"><m>g_1(y) = y^2</m></label>
<label at="c2-g2-label" anchor="(0.72,0.22)" alignment="se"
    color="#0072B2"><m>g_2(y) = y^{1/3}</m></label>
<label at="c2-top-label" anchor="(0.52,0.54)" alignment="n">
<m>y = 0.5</m>
</label>
<label at="c2-bottom-label" anchor="(0.24,0.06)" alignment="s">
<m>y = 0.1</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A region between two curves for y between zero point one and zero point five. The left boundary, drawn in vermillion, is g 1 of y equals y squared. The right boundary, drawn in blue, is g 2 of y equals the cube root of y. The region between them is shaded light green with three horizontal green strips, and dashed lines mark y equals zero point one and y equals zero point five.">
  <annotation ref="c2-g1" text="The left boundary g 1 of y equals y squared, drawn in vermillion."/>
  <annotation ref="c2-g2" text="The right boundary g 2 of y equals y to the one third, drawn in blue."/>
  <annotation ref="c2-region" text="The region R, shaded in light green."/>
  <annotation ref="c2-strip2" text="A typical horizontal strip at a fixed y. The inner integral in x runs along this strip from the left curve to the right curve."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The idea behind both cases<mdash/>sweeping a strip across the
region while the inner integral measures the length of the
strip<mdash/>is animated in <xref ref="fig-strip-sweep-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-strip-sweep-video">
<caption>
Animation of iterated integration over a general region. For the
region of <xref ref="example-case1-vertical"/>, a vertical strip at
position <m>x</m> has height <m>f_1(x) - f_2(x)</m>; as the strip
sweeps from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 1</m>, the outer integral
accumulates the strip contributions, arriving at
<m>\iint_R (2xy+1)\,dA = \frac{15}{8}</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-strip-sweep"
       source="videos/strip_sweep_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-tetrahedron-volume">
<title>Volume of a Tetrahedron</title>

<example xml:id="example-tetrahedron">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the volume of the tetrahedron bounded by the planes
<m>x + 2y + z = 2</m>, <m>x = 2y</m>, <m>x = 0</m> and <m>z = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
<xref ref="fig-tetrahedron-tikz"/> shows the tetrahedron <m>T</m>
bounded by the planes <m>x + 2y + z = 2</m>, <m>x = 2y</m>,
<m>x = 0</m> and <m>z = 0</m>. To find the volume of the tetrahedron
<m>T</m>, we first need to figure out the region of integration.
<xref ref="fig-tetrahedron-base-prefigure"/> shows the region of
integration, i.e., the base of the tetrahedron. Therefore, our region
of integration is
</p>

<me>
R = \left\{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 1,\
\frac{x}{2} \le y \le \left(1 - \frac{x}{2}\right)\right\}.
</me>

<p>
For the volume <m>V</m> of the tetrahedron, we integrate the height
<m>z = 2 - x - 2y</m> of the top plane over <m>R</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
V \amp= \iint_R (2 - x - 2y)\,dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^1\!\!\int_{x/2}^{1 - x/2} (2 - x - 2y)\,dy\,dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^1\left[\left.\left(2y - xy - y^2\right)
\right|_{y=x/2}^{y=1-x/2}\right]dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^1\left(2\left(1-\frac{x}{2}\right)
- x\left(1-\frac{x}{2}\right)
- \left(1-\frac{x}{2}\right)^2\right.
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\qquad \left. - 2\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)
+ x\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)
+ \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^2\right)dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^1\left(x^2 - 2x + 1\right)dx
= \left.\left(\frac{x^3}{3} - x^2 + x\right)\right|_0^1
= \frac{1}{3}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-tetrahedron-tikz">
<caption>
The tetrahedron <m>T</m> bounded by the planes <m>x + 2y + z = 2</m>
(slanted face), <m>x = 2y</m> (vertical face through the
<m>z</m>-axis), <m>x = 0</m> (the <m>yz</m>-plane) and <m>z = 0</m>
(the base). Its vertices are <m>(0,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,0,2)</m>,
<m>(0,1,0)</m> and <m>\left(1,\tfrac12,0\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a tetrahedron drawn in translucent
blue. One vertex is at the origin, one at height two on the z axis,
one at zero one zero on the y axis, and one at the point one comma one
half comma zero. The slanted face x plus two y plus z equals two and
the vertical face x equals two y are labeled with arrows.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={97}{20},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=1.5, ymin=0, ymax=1.5, zmin=0, zmax=2.4,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % vertices: O(0,0,0), A(0,0,2), B(0,1,0), C(1,0.5,0)
    % ---- back face x = 0 : O, A, B ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.15]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:0,1,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- base z = 0 : O, B, C ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.22]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,1,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- face x = 2y : O, A, C ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.32]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- slanted face x + 2y + z = 2 : A, B, C ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.45]
      (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:0,1,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- edges ----
    \draw[oiblue!70!black, line width=1.0pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,2)
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,1,0)
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0)
      (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:0,1,0)
      (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0)
      (axis cs:0,1,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0);
    % ---- vertices and labels ----
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.6pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(0,0,2) (0,1,0) (1,0.5,0)};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.02,0.06,2.05)
      {$(0,0,2)$};
    \node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0,1.03,0.08)
      {$(0,1,0)$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.05,0.5,-0.1)
      {$\left(1,\tfrac12,0\right)$};
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.02,-0.04,-0.02)
      {$0$};
    \node[font=\large] at (axis cs:0.28,0.55,0.35) {$T$};
    % ---- face labels with arrows ----
    \draw[-{Latex}, black!70, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0.75,0.15,1.4) -- (axis cs:0.42,0.35,0.9);
    \node[anchor=south, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.75,0.15,1.42)
      {$x = 2y$};
    \draw[-{Latex}, black!70, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0.45,1.05,1.35) -- (axis cs:0.3,0.62,1.0);
    \node[anchor=south, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.45,1.05,1.37)
      {$x + 2y + z = 2$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-tetrahedron-base-prefigure">
<caption>
The region of integration for <xref ref="example-tetrahedron"/>: the
base of the tetrahedron in the <m>xy</m>-plane, bounded below by
<m>y = x/2</m> and above by <m>x + 2y = 2</m> (or <m>y = 1 - x/2</m>)
for <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>. A typical vertical strip is drawn in blue.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-tetrahedron-base">
<diagram dimensions="(460,300)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (t, 1 - t/2)</definition>
<definition>bot(t) = (t, t/2)</definition>
<definition>topsc(t) = 1 - t/2</definition>
<definition>botsc(t) = t/2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.55,2.75,1.55)">
<axes at="tb-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<area-between-curves at="tb-region" function1="botsc" function2="topsc"
    domain="(0,1)" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none"/>
<vector at="tb-strip" v="(0,1.85)" tail="(0.4,-0.45)" stroke="#D55E00"
    thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="tb-top" function="top" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="tb-bot" function="bot" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="tb-left" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="#555555"
    thickness="3"/>
<point at="tb-upper" p="(0.4,0.8)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="tb-lower" p="(0.4,0.2)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="tb-corner" p="(1,0.5)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>\left(1,\tfrac12\right)</m>
</point>
<point at="tb-one" p="(0,1)" fill="black" alignment="w">
<m>1</m>
</point>
<label at="tb-top-label" anchor="(0.02,1.18)" alignment="ne"
    color="#0072B2">
<m>x + 2y = 2</m>
</label>
<label at="tb-bot-label" anchor="(0.78,0.24)" alignment="se"
    color="#009E73">
<m>y = x/2</m>
</label>
<label at="tb-origin" anchor="(-0.06,-0.06)" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</label>
<rectangle at="tb-legend" lower-left="(1.38,0.55)" dimensions="(1.32,0.75)"
    stroke="black" fill="white" thickness="1"/>
<point at="tb-leg-upper" p="(1.5,1.1)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<label at="tb-leg-upper-label" anchor="(1.58,1.1)" alignment="e"
    color="#0072B2">upper bound for <m>y</m></label>
<point at="tb-leg-lower" p="(1.5,0.75)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="tb-leg-lower-label" anchor="(1.58,0.75)" alignment="e"
    color="#009E73">lower bound for <m>y</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A triangle in the x y plane with vertices at the origin, at zero comma one on the y axis, and at one comma one half. The upper edge, in blue, is the line x plus two y equals two; the lower edge, in green, is the line y equals x over two; the left edge lies on the y axis. A vermillion vertical arrow at x equals zero point four runs from the very bottom of the graph to near the top, crossing the lower edge at a green point and the upper edge at a blue point. A legend box explains that the blue point is the upper bound for y and the green point is the lower bound for y.">
  <annotation ref="tb-region" text="The base of the tetrahedron: the region of integration R, shaded tan."/>
  <annotation ref="tb-strip" text="A vertical arrow at a fixed x, running from below the region to above it. It enters the region at the lower bound and leaves it at the upper bound."/>
  <annotation ref="tb-lower" text="The green intersection point with the line y equals x over 2: the lower bound for y."/>
  <annotation ref="tb-upper" text="The blue intersection point with the line x plus 2 y equals 2: the upper bound for y."/>
  <annotation ref="tb-legend" text="Legend: the blue point marks the upper bound for y and the green point marks the lower bound for y."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<remark xml:id="remark-reverse-order">
<title>Why not integrate in the reverse order?</title>
<p>
We could also try to integrate with respect to <m>x</m> first, using
horizontal strips. However, as the figure below shows, the right end of
a horizontal strip depends on where the strip is: for
<m>y \le \tfrac12</m> the strip ends on the line <m>x = 2y</m>, while
for <m>y \ge \tfrac12</m> it ends on the line <m>x + 2y = 2</m> (that
is, <m>x = 2 - 2y</m>). We would therefore have to split the region
into two pieces and evaluate two double integrals, so integrating with
respect to <m>y</m> first is the more efficient choice here.
</p>

<sidebyside width="60%">
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-tetrahedron-base-reverse">
<diagram dimensions="(420,300)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (t, 1 - t/2)</definition>
<definition>bot(t) = (t, t/2)</definition>
<definition>topsc(t) = 1 - t/2</definition>
<definition>botsc(t) = t/2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.4,2.3,1.55)">
<axes at="rv-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<area-between-curves at="rv-region" function1="botsc" function2="topsc"
    domain="(0,1)" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none"/>
<vector at="rv-strip1" v="(1.35,0)" tail="(-0.4,0.3)" stroke="#D55E00"
    thickness="3"/>
<vector at="rv-strip2" v="(1.25,0)" tail="(-0.4,0.75)" stroke="#CC79A7"
    thickness="3"/>
<line at="rv-split" endpoints="((0,0.5),(1.15,0.5))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="rv-top" function="top" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="rv-bot" function="bot" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="rv-left" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="#555555"
    thickness="3"/>
<point at="rv-end1" p="(0.6,0.3)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="rv-end2" p="(0.5,0.75)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="rv-corner" p="(1,0.5)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>\left(1,\tfrac12\right)</m>
</point>
<label at="rv-split-label" anchor="(0.06,0.47)" alignment="se">
<m>y = \tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="rv-top-label" anchor="(0.02,1.18)" alignment="ne"
    color="#0072B2"><m>x = 2 - 2y</m></label>
<label at="rv-bot-label" anchor="(0.72,0.2)" alignment="se"
    color="#009E73"><m>x = 2y</m></label>
<label at="rv-origin" anchor="(-0.06,-0.06)" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same triangular region with two horizontal arrows. A vermillion arrow at height y equals zero point three crosses the region and its right end lies on the green line x equals two y. A reddish purple arrow at height y equals zero point seven five crosses the region and its right end lies on the blue line x equals two minus two y. A dashed line at y equals one half separates the two cases.">
  <annotation ref="rv-strip1" text="A horizontal strip below y equals one half. Its right end lies on the line x equals 2 y."/>
  <annotation ref="rv-strip2" text="A horizontal strip above y equals one half. Its right end lies on the line x equals 2 minus 2 y."/>
  <annotation ref="rv-split" text="The dashed line y equals one half where the right boundary changes from one line to the other, forcing a split into two integrals."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</remark>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-area-by-double-integration">
<title>Area by Double Integration</title>

<p>
In the previous session, we talked about finding the double integral of
the form <m>\iint_R f(x,y)\,dx\,dy</m> and how it corresponds to the
volume of an object with base <m>R</m> and height <m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
This session, we will look at a special case of this double integral,
which corresponds to the area of region <m>R</m>:
</p>

<me>
\text{Area of region } R = \iint_R dx\,dy = \iint_R dA.
</me>

<example xml:id="example-area-double-integration">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the area of the region <m>R</m> shown in
<xref ref="fig-area-region-prefigure"/>, bounded on the right by
<m>x = y - y^3</m>, on the left by <m>x = -1</m>, and by the parabola
<m>y = (x+1)^2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<title>Method 1</title>
<p>
We split the region <m>R</m> into two regions <m>R_1</m> and
<m>R_2</m> as follows:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
R_1 \amp= \{(x,y) : -1 \le y \le 0,\ -1 \le x \le y - y^3\},
</mrow>
<mrow>
R_2 \amp= \{(x,y) : 0 \le y \le 1,\ \sqrt{y} - 1 \le x \le y - y^3\}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Note that on <m>R_2</m>, the left boundary is the parabola
<m>y = (x+1)^2</m>, solved for <m>x</m> as <m>x = \sqrt{y} - 1</m>.
Then
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\text{Area} \amp= \iint_R dx\,dy
= \iint_{R_1} dx\,dy + \iint_{R_2} dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_{-1}^{0}\!\!\int_{-1}^{y-y^3} dx\,dy
+ \int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{\sqrt y - 1}^{y-y^3} dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_{-1}^{0}\left(y - y^3 + 1\right)dy
+ \int_{0}^{1}\left(y - y^3 - \sqrt y + 1\right)dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_{-1}^{1}\left(y - y^3 + 1\right)dy
- \int_{0}^{1}\sqrt y\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \left.\left(\frac{y^2}{2} - \frac{y^4}{4} + y\right)
\right|_{-1}^{1}
- \left.\left(\frac{2}{3}y^{3/2}\right)\right|_0^1
= 2 - \frac{2}{3} = \frac{4}{3}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<solution>
<title>Method 2 (using symmetry)</title>
<p>
The curve <m>x = y - y^3</m> is <em>odd</em>: rotating it by
<m>180^\circ</m> about the origin maps it onto itself. As shown in
<xref ref="fig-area-symmetry-prefigure"/>, this rotation carries the
piece <m>A</m> that the curve cuts <em>out of</em> the unit square
<m>[-1,0]\times[-1,0]</m> exactly onto the piece <m>A</m> that the
curve adds <em>to the right of</em> the <m>y</m>-axis for
<m>0 \le y \le 1</m>. These two pieces therefore have the same area,
and swapping one for the other turns the region into the unit square
(of area <m>1</m>) together with the strip between the parabola
<m>y = (x+1)^2</m> and the <m>y</m>-axis. Hence
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\text{Area} \amp= \underbrace{1}_{\text{square}}
+ \underbrace{\int_0^1 \big(0 - (\sqrt y - 1)\big)\,dy}_{\text{strip}}
= 1 + \int_0^1\left(1 - \sqrt y\right)dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 1 + \left.\left(y - \frac{2}{3}y^{3/2}\right)\right|_0^1
= 1 + \frac{1}{3} = \frac{4}{3}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-area-symmetry-prefigure">
<caption>
Method 2: since <m>x = y - y^3</m> is odd, the piece <m>A</m> removed
from the unit square (below the <m>x</m>-axis) is congruent to the
piece <m>A</m> added to the right of the <m>y</m>-axis (above it).
The area is therefore the area of the square, <m>1</m>, plus the area
<m>\frac13</m> of the green strip between the parabola
<m>y = (x+1)^2</m> and the <m>y</m>-axis.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
