<subsection xml:id="subsec-general-regions-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
Both problems below ask only for a <em>setup</em>: a double integral whose
value is the volume or the area in question, with a region of integration
that is no longer a rectangle. Each figure can be rotated by dragging the
background, and zoomed by scrolling.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-cylinder-sphere">
<title>A ball cut by a cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral for computing the volume of the region bounded by
the cylinder <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m> and the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m>. See <xref ref="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The solid is the part of the ball that lies inside the cylinder. It is
bounded below by <m>z=-\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m> and above by
<m>z=\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>, so its height over a point of the region of
integration is <m>2\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m>.
</p>
<p>
The region of integration <m>D</m> is the shadow of the solid: the points
lying inside both circles <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> and <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m>.
Those circles cross where <m>\sqrt{1-x^2}=1-\sqrt{1-x^2}</m>, that is at
<m>x=\pm\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>, <m>y=\frac12</m>. Between those values of
<m>x</m>, the lower boundary of <m>D</m> is the cylinder and the upper
boundary is the sphere, so
</p>
<me>
V = \iint_D 2\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}\,dA
  = \int_{-\sqrt3/2}^{\sqrt3/2}\!\!
    \int_{1-\sqrt{1-x^2}}^{\sqrt{1-x^2}}
    2\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}\;dy\,dx .
</me>
<p>
This is a region of the kind this section is about: <m>D</m> is bounded
above and below by the graphs of functions of <m>x</m>, which is
<xref ref="sec-general-regions-case1"/>. In polar coordinates the same
integral becomes far friendlier still.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere">
<caption>
Figure (4): the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m> and the cylinder
<m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m>. Use this graph to find the region of integration
and the integrand. The wall of the cylinder runs straight down to the
<m>xy</m>-plane, where the red circle outlines the region of
integration.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="z7qbvdyz"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. The black
curve is where the sphere and the cylinder meet; the region of
integration is the part of the <m>xy</m>-plane lying inside both the red
circle and the shadow of the sphere.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-area-arcsin">
<title>Problem: an area as a double integral</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral for computing the area of the region bounded by
the blue curve and the red curve, which are graphed in
<xref ref="fig-ws-arcsin-region"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The red curve is <m>y=\sin^{-1}(x)</m>, that is <m>x=\sin y</m> for
<m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \le y \le \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, and the blue curve is the
half of the circle <m>x^2+y^2=R^2</m>, <m>R^2 = 1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}</m>,
that runs counterclockwise from <m>(1,\frac{\pi}{2})</m> to
<m>(-1,-\frac{\pi}{2})</m>. Those two endpoints are the ends of the red
curve, and they are antipodal on the circle.
</p>
<p>
Slice the region horizontally. For <m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \le y \le
\frac{\pi}{2}</m> a slice runs from the circle on the left to the red
curve on the right; above <m>y=\frac{\pi}{2}</m> it runs from the circle
to the circle. Since area is <m>\iint_D dA</m>,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
A \amp = \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{R^2-y^2}}^{\ \sin y}
        dx\,dy
      + \int_{\pi/2}^{R}\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{R^2-y^2}}^{\ \sqrt{R^2-y^2}}
        dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\left(\sin y + \sqrt{R^2-y^2}\right) dy
     + \int_{\pi/2}^{R} 2\sqrt{R^2-y^2}\,dy .
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
It is worth seeing what comes out. The first integrand contains
<m>\sin y</m>, which is odd, so it contributes nothing, and what is left
is exactly the integral of <m>2\sqrt{R^2-y^2}</m> over
<m>-R \le y \le R</m>, cut into two pieces. The area is therefore half
the area of the disk:
</p>
<me>
A = \frac{\pi R^2}{2}
  = \frac{\pi}{2}\left(1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}\right) \approx 5.447 .
</me>
<p>
The wiggle of the red curve costs nothing, because it bulges as far to
one side of the diameter through its endpoints as it does to the other.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-arcsin-region">
<caption>
Figure (5): the region bounded by the blue arc of the circle
<m>x^2+y^2=1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}</m> and the red curve
<m>y=\sin^{-1}(x)</m>. The two curves meet at
<m>\left(1,\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-1,-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>, which are opposite ends of a
diameter; the dashed arc is the rest of the circle.
</caption>
